 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSocket connections that the server owns end to end, rather than HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service: the market accepts the added connection-capacity and reconnect-handling work in exchange for fast updates with no recurring realtime-vendor fee or provider coupling. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around this choice. The change store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication so a reconnecting session can catch up; delivery owns WebSocket session lifecycle; browsers carry reconnect and catch-up logic for gaps in the socket stream.
 
